C = [
("h1", "12. The Guests: Friends of the Bridegroom"),
("p", "Claim: the marriage supper has guests, and the guests are not the wife. The Scriptures give them names that keep them apart from her: the friend of the bridegroom, the children of the bridechamber, those who are bidden, those who are called, the servants who wait for their lord. The volume holds that they are the faithful of Israel’s prophetic hope who are called to the table, and that the Body of Christ is neither the wife nor a guest, because it is not in the scenes of the book. The text does not list the guests, and the identification rests on the pattern the passages set, which this chapter sets out."),
("sub", "12.1 Two parties in one verse pair"),
("p", "Revelation 19 names two parties in two verses. The first is the wife, who “hath made herself ready” (Revelation 19:7). The second is those who are called: “Blessed are they which are called unto the marriage supper of the Lamb” (Revelation 19:9). A wedding supper is attended by guests, and a bride does not receive an invitation to her own wedding. The text itself separates the one who is married from the ones who are called to sit at the table. A reading that makes the wife and the called the same company must explain why the angel speaks of them in two ways. Thomas Constable’s //Expository Notes// (2012) read the wife as the church, and yet they say, “Those invited to the supper will include the bride and other believers who are not members of the church.” On his reading the called include persons beyond the wife, and so the verse already treats the wife and the called as two companies. The volume differs from him on who the wife is, and agrees that the passage has more than one party. John Walvoord’s //The Revelation of Jesus Christ// says the same of the verse: “the wife of the Lamb is distinguished from the attendants at the wedding.” He takes the attendants to be saints of other ages. John MacArthur’s sermon on Revelation 19:7 to 10 presses the same point from the supper: “She’s not an invited guest, she’s the bride.” He then names Abraham, Isaac, Jacob and the prophets among the invited and says of them, “They’re not the church, they’re a part of redeemed Israel.” His guests are the volume’s guests, and the volume differs from him only on who the wife is. The reply to those who make the two one is that the passage does not say so. It names the wife by one verb and the called by another, and the two verses come one after the other."),
("sub", "12.2 The pattern guest"),
("p", "John the Baptist is the clearest case in the Gospels of what a friend of the bridegroom is. He “standeth and heareth him, rejoiceth greatly because of the bridegroom’s voice” (John 3:29). He is sent “before him” (John 3:28), he is not the Christ, and he is not the one who has the bride. He is of Israel’s prophetic line, he is faithful to the end of his own testimony, and he rejoices in a wedding that is not his. The friend is a person who shares the bridegroom’s joy and who has no claim to the bride’s place."),
("p", "The Lord uses the same company in his own words: the “children of the bridechamber” who do not mourn while the bridegroom is with them (Matthew 9:15). In the parable of the king’s son the guests are those who were “bidden” and then others who were gathered in (Matthew 22:3, 9–10). In the parable of the great supper the master “bade many” and sent his servant at supper time to say, “Come; for all things are now ready” (Luke 14:16–17). In Luke 12 the servants wait for the lord who returns from the wedding and are blessed if they are found watching (Luke 12:36–37). Every one of these is a party beside the bridegroom, who is called, who waits or who is bidden, and none of them is the wife."),
("sub", "12.3 Who the guests are"),
("p", "The volume’s answer is that the guests are the faithful of the prophets’ hope who are called to the supper, which is to say the saints of Israel’s prophetic line who looked for the kingdom and who stand with the bridegroom as John did. The reasons are three. The friends and the bidden belong to the world of the prophets, in which John, the king’s first servants and the invited guests are all found. The parables that speak of the supper are set within the kingdom of heaven and the kingdom of God (Matthew 22:2; 25:1; Luke 14:15). And the supper of Revelation 19:9 is announced in the same book that names the wife by her tribes, so that the table and the wife belong to the same hope."),
("p", "The text does not give a roll of the guests, and the volume does not invent one. The pattern points to the faithful of the prophets’ line, but the verses do not name them, and a reader who finds the pattern less certain than the volume does may still hold the main position, that the wife and the guests are two parties. The volume does hold one thing without qualification. The Body of Christ is not the wife, and it is not a guest at this table. The Body is not found among the parties of the scene, and its seat is in the heavenly places (Ephesians 2:6)."),
("sub", "12.4 Two suppers in one chapter"),
("p", "Revelation 19 contains two suppers, and they are very different. The first is blessed: “Blessed are they which are called unto the marriage supper of the Lamb” (Revelation 19:9). The second is a gathering of birds. An angel standing in the sun cries to the fowls, “Come and gather yourselves together unto the supper of the great God; that ye may eat the flesh of kings, and the flesh of captains, and the flesh of mighty men” (Revelation 19:17–18). One is a table of rejoicing, and the other is the end of the nations’ armies. The two are set in one chapter on either side of the rider, and they show the two outcomes of the one return. The second supper belongs to the account of the return and the war. The first is the guests’ and the wife’s."),
("tbl", "The table follows the parties and the two suppers.", [
    ("John 3:29", "“the friend of the bridegroom, which standeth and heareth him, rejoiceth greatly”", "The friend stands beside the bridegroom and is not the bride"),
    ("Matt 9:15", "“Can the children of the bridechamber mourn, as long as the bridegroom is with them?”", "The companions of the bridegroom are a wedding company"),
    ("Matt 22:3", "“to call them that were bidden to the wedding”", "The first guests are the bidden"),
    ("Luke 14:17", "“to say to them that were bidden, Come; for all things are now ready”", "The supper is ready and the bidden are told"),
    ("Luke 12:36", "“men that wait for their lord, when he will return from the wedding”", "The servants wait and are blessed"),
    ("Rev 19:7", "“his wife hath made herself ready”", "The wife is the one who is wed"),
    ("Rev 19:9", "“Blessed are they which are called unto the marriage supper of the Lamb”", "The called are blessed guests"),
    ("Rev 19:17", "“Come and gather yourselves together unto the supper of the great God”", "The second supper is a gathering of fowls"),
  ], [0.14, 0.52, 0.34]),
("sub", "12.5 What this chapter does not say"),
("bul", [
    "It does not say that every guest is named in a verse. It says that the verses name the parties and that the wife is one and the called are another.",
    "It does not say that the guests are lesser than the wife. They are blessed, and the supper is the Lamb’s.",
    "It does not say that the wedding of Matthew 22 and the marriage supper of Revelation 19 are one event. The parables teach the call and readiness, and the Revelation announces the marriage.",
  ]),
("call", "Where the argument stands", "The wife is wed, and the called are blessed guests. The friend of the bridegroom, the children of the bridechamber and the bidden are the parties the Scriptures set beside the bridegroom. The volume holds that they are the faithful of Israel’s prophetic hope and that the Body is neither wife nor guest."),
]
